2025-05-01 01:58:20 - Thread-584 (execute) - INFO - [Exploration]
Relevant columns & their potential use for the task  

• "BLOCK_TIMESTAMP" : filter events/logs that happened after 2023‑01‑01.  
• "BLOCK_NUMBER"    : alternative height filter (if a height that corresponds to ≥ 2023‑01‑01 is known).  
• "ADDRESS"         : contract / sender address that emitted the log or event.  
• "ARGS"            : JSON‑encoded, may contain a “to” address (e.g. Transfer events).  
• "TOPICS"          : indexed parameters; can also hold a “to” address in position 2 of an ERC‑20 Transfer.  
• "BLOCK_HASH"      : for possible joins / integrity checks.  
• "BLOCK_TIMESTAMP" & “BLOCK_NUMBER” : for block‑size or date filtering, even though physical block size is not stored.  
• "TRANSACTION_HASH": to count how many times a given sender/contract appears.  

Typical conditions to appear in the WHERE clause  

• "BLOCK_TIMESTAMP" > 1672531200000000        -- 2023‑01‑01 (micro‑seconds)  
• "ARGS" IS NOT NULL                          -- must contain decoded arguments  
• "EVENT_SIGNATURE" ILIKE 'Transfer%'         -- focus on transfers that have “to” in ARGS  
• "TOPICS"[1] IS NOT NULL                     -- non‑null indexed “to” parameter  
• "ADDRESS" IS NOT NULL  
• "REMOVED" = FALSE  

Below are ten exploratory Snowflake SQL queries (simple → progressively richer).  
All column names are enclosed in double‑quotes and every query ends with LIMIT 20.  

```sql
-- 1) Description: inspect raw logs emitted after 1‑Jan‑2023.  
SELECT "BLOCK_NUMBER",
       "BLOCK_TIMESTAMP",
       "ADDRESS",
       "TOPICS",
       "DATA"
FROM   GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN.GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN_ARBITRUM_ONE_US."LOGS"
WHERE  "BLOCK_TIMESTAMP" > 1672531200000000
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 2) Description: list distinct event signatures (decoded) observed since 1‑Jan‑2023.  
SELECT DISTINCT "EVENT_SIGNATURE"
FROM   GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN.GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN_ARBITRUM_ONE_US."DECODED_EVENTS"
WHERE  "BLOCK_TIMESTAMP" > 1672531200000000
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 3) Description: sample 20 decoded Transfer‑like events (signatures containing ‘Transfer’).  
SELECT "TRANSACTION_HASH",
       "LOG_INDEX",
       "ADDRESS",
       "EVENT_SIGNATURE",
       "ARGS"
FROM   GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN.GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN_ARBITRUM_ONE_US."DECODED_EVENTS"
WHERE  "BLOCK_TIMESTAMP" > 1672531200000000
  AND  "EVENT_SIGNATURE" ILIKE 'Transfer%'
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 4) Description: flatten ARGS of Transfer events to expose from / to / value fields.  
SELECT  d."TRANSACTION_HASH",
        idx.value::VARIANT[0]::STRING  AS "FROM_ADDR",
        idx.value::VARIANT[1]::STRING  AS "TO_ADDR",
        idx.value::VARIANT[2]::STRING  AS "AMOUNT"
FROM    GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN.GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN_ARBITRUM_ONE_US."DECODED_EVENTS" d,
        LATERAL FLATTEN( input => PARSE_JSON(d."ARGS") ) idx
WHERE   d."BLOCK_TIMESTAMP" > 1672531200000000
  AND   d."EVENT_SIGNATURE" ILIKE 'Transfer%'
LIMIT   20;
```

```sql
-- 5) Description: count how many times each contract address emitted a Transfer event in 2023+.  
SELECT  "ADDRESS",
        COUNT(*) AS "EVENT_COUNT"
FROM    GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN.GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN_ARBITRUM_ONE_US."DECODED_EVENTS"
WHERE   "BLOCK_TIMESTAMP" > 1672531200000000
  AND   "EVENT_SIGNATURE" ILIKE 'Transfer%'
GROUP BY "ADDRESS"
ORDER BY "EVENT_COUNT" DESC
LIMIT   20;
```

```sql
-- 6) Description: derive candidate “to” addresses directly from indexed topic #3 (ERC‑20 Transfer).  
SELECT  "TOPICS"[2]::STRING        AS "TO_TOPIC_ADDR",
        COUNT(*)                   AS "OCCURRENCES"
FROM    GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN.GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN_ARBITRUM_ONE_US."DECODED_EVENTS"
WHERE   "BLOCK_TIMESTAMP" > 1672531200000000
  AND   "EVENT_SIGNATURE" = 'Transfer(address,address,uint256)'
GROUP BY "TOPICS"[2]
ORDER BY "OCCURRENCES" DESC
LIMIT   20;
```

```sql
-- 7) Description: same as #6 but require non‑null “to” and show the originating sender contract.  
SELECT  "ADDRESS"                 AS "SENDER_CONTRACT",
        "TOPICS"[2]::STRING       AS "TO_TOPIC_ADDR",
        COUNT(*)                  AS "N_TX"
FROM    GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN.GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN_ARBITRUM_ONE_US."DECODED_EVENTS"
WHERE   "BLOCK_TIMESTAMP" > 1672531200000000
  AND   "EVENT_SIGNATURE" = 'Transfer(address,address,uint256)'
  AND   "TOPICS"[2]      IS NOT NULL
GROUP BY "ADDRESS", "TOPICS"[2]
ORDER BY "N_TX" DESC
LIMIT   20;
```

```sql
-- 8) Description: peak at logs whose data length suggests very large transactions (DATA > 4096 bytes).  
SELECT  "BLOCK_NUMBER",
        "TRANSACTION_HASH",
        "ADDRESS",
        BYTELENGTH("DATA") AS "DATA_SIZE_BYTES"
FROM    GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN.GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN_ARBITRUM_ONE_US."LOGS"
WHERE   "BLOCK_TIMESTAMP" > 1672531200000000
  AND   BYTELENGTH("DATA") > 4096
LIMIT   20;
```

```sql
-- 9) Description: correlate decoded Transfer events with their raw log to view both sides.  
WITH ev AS (
  SELECT "TRANSACTION_HASH","LOG_INDEX","EVENT_SIGNATURE","ARGS"
  FROM   GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN.GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN_ARBITRUM_ONE_US."DECODED_EVENTS"
  WHERE  "BLOCK_TIMESTAMP" > 1672531200000000
    AND  "EVENT_SIGNATURE" = 'Transfer(address,address,uint256)'
)
SELECT  l."BLOCK_NUMBER",
        l."TRANSACTION_INDEX",
        l."ADDRESS"         AS "LOG_ADDRESS",
        ev."ARGS"
FROM    GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN.GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN_ARBITRUM_ONE_US."LOGS"  l
JOIN    ev
  ON    l."TRANSACTION_HASH" = ev."TRANSACTION_HASH"
 AND    l."LOG_INDEX"        = ev."LOG_INDEX"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10) Description: approximate “most frequent sender contract” since 2023 by counting logs size > 4096 bytes.  
SELECT  "ADDRESS"                       AS "SUSPECT_SENDER",
        COUNT(*)                        AS "BIG_TX_LOG_COUNT"
FROM    GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN.GOOG_BLOCKCHAIN_ARBITRUM_ONE_US."LOGS"
WHERE   "BLOCK_TIMESTAMP" > 1672531200000000
  AND   BYTELENGTH("DATA") > 4096
GROUP BY "ADDRESS"
ORDER BY "BIG_TX_LOG_COUNT" DESC
LIMIT   20;
```

These ten queries progress from simple inspection of logs to counting high‑volume senders and extracting “to” addresses. They provide a step‑by‑step framework for identifying the address most frequently acting as sender in large‑sized blocks since 2023.
